1078 #edb/ref 17592186447268 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447294 1079 #uuid "b8afcb64-7f3e-534f-a7ec-9869fa2663b7" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447293 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447295 1069 "q-83852" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447295 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447295 1072 #uuid "337615da-7bae-5da8-aef5-ed48be968edc" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447295 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=y-3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y(\\frac{1}{3}-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=3,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447292 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447296 1069 "q-83851" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447296 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447296 1072 #uuid "3b37913a-69c1-5101-b52b-7d6c5fd60a19" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447296 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=55y-11{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=11y(5-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=11,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5.$" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447288 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447297 1069 "q-119267" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447297 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447297 1072 #uuid "47725b1d-6489-53a4-9d3a-6bd1c0f75d94" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447297 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=35y-7{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=7y(5-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=7,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5.$" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447289 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447298 1069 "q-83850" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447298 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447298 1072 #uuid "52ad75c1-24f1-5969-9d6c-0ceaa8d872d2" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447298 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=20y-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(10-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10.$" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447290 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447299 1069 "q-85975" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447299 1071 "Some viral content on the internet starts spreading through an audience of $60\\,000$ people. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ who have seen the content is jointly proportional to the number of people who have seen the content at time $t,$ measured in hours, and the number who have not. If the constant of proportionality is $0.75,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447299 1072 #uuid "6009a1c0-18f1-5130-b32c-9c18394dafe6" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447299 1074 "Since the growth rate of people who have seen the content is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have seen the content at time $t,$ and the number who have not, the number of people who have seen the content follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.75,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=60\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who have seen the content is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.75P(60\\,000-P).\n$$" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447291 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447300 1069 "q-83855" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447300 1071 "A rumor is spreading in a school of $2\\,000$ students. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ who have heard the rumor is jointly proportional to the number of people who have heard the rumor at time $t,$ measured in hours, and the number who have not heard the rumor. If the constant of proportionality is $2.5,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447300 1072 #uuid "cfc9e704-11ab-5799-a5b1-c0cc751bf228" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447300 1074 "Since the rumor's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have heard the rumor at time $t,$ and the number who have not, the number of people who have heard the rumor follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=2.5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=2\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who heard the rumor is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2.5P(2\\,000-P).\n$$" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447294 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447301 1069 "q-83862" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447301 1071 "The growth rate of the woodpecker population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the woodpecker population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the woodpecker population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,1$ and the maximum possible woodpecker population is $10\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447301 1072 #uuid "e56756c2-a487-52f8-beba-b87d8eaed329" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true] [17592186447301 1074 "Since the woodpecker population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the woodpecker population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the woodpecker population, the woodpecker population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,1,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000.$ Therefore, the woodpecker population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,1P(10\\,000-P).\n$$" 13194139534996 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-076adf41-55fa-5e02-a5f4-92e1a61c4181" 17592186447253 "answer-14a10537-d01c-588a-af50-1725a012a2bf" 17592186447254 "answer-19c12834-371c-5f63-bdfb-02c2541583fe" 17592186447255 "answer-1d5f4efc-0abf-57f1-ba32-0185947e100a" 17592186447256 "answer-1f22fbf4-e2a1-5430-b987-ad309011b129" 17592186447257 "answer-25935ef1-27a2-5178-a4d2-d936bbae9e34" 17592186447258 "answer-26312008-2455-55a8-bc1d-9b869b44fda8" 17592186447259 "answer-2734d77f-afd6-5eac-babe-fd41a107ee1c" 17592186447260 "answer-2c745c03-98a8-571c-aa79-f32d40ebf4e1" 17592186447261 "answer-36f03a9c-6c86-514e-8536-0c6bba5a3abf" 17592186447262 "answer-3ebf0f82-e343-52b7-8a49-05d015118207" 17592186447263 "answer-4ccb0db6-5ea4-55e4-8c9d-e19190d4f9ec" 17592186447264 "answer-5a58cff2-1268-5a47-8339-f8986d6b3b53" 17592186447265 "answer-5e2458c3-7905-560b-b228-5e789283c915" 17592186447266 "answer-74b013fb-7bc8-5cdf-9d89-9d9560979c6d" 17592186447267 "answer-76b57d70-a086-5a76-ad08-1757bf1a92fd" 17592186447268 "answer-899816c6-722e-5949-9ce4-1a89698b454a" 17592186447269 "answer-94d0e760-c927-55f9-8532-b3496e6146ba" 17592186447270 "answer-9d888226-313c-58ec-b603-27ecf200a67a" 17592186447271 "answer-a1bd8bc3-8421-52dd-bc14-38e7879c5978" 17592186447272 "answer-a2fabba3-d0eb-5536-9a95-97abc2710798" 17592186447273 "answer-a406afc4-d960-5